text."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Generally speaking, you should just `derive` a `Debug` implementation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When used with the alternate format specifier `#?`, the output is pretty-printed."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For more information on formatters, see [the module-level documentation][module]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [module]: ../../std/fmt/index.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This trait can be used with `#[derive]` if all fields implement `Debug`. When"},{"DocComment":" `derive`d for structs, it will use the name of the `struct`, then `{`, then a"},{"DocComment":" comma-separated list of each field's name and `Debug` value, then `}`. For"},{"DocComment":" `enum`s, it will use the name of the variant and, if applicable, `(`, then the"},{"DocComment":" `Debug` values of the fields, then `)`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Stability"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Derived `Debug` formats are not stable, and so may change with future Rust"},{"DocComment":" versions. Additionally, `Debug` implementations of types provided by the"},{"DocComment":" standard library (`std`, `core`, `alloc`, etc.) are not stable, and"},{"DocComment":" may also change with future Rust versions."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Deriving an implementation:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" #[derive(Debug)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Point {"},{"DocComment":"     x: i32,"},{"DocComment":"     y: i32,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let origin = Point { x: 0, y: 0 };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!("},{"DocComment":"     format!("The origin is: {origin:?}"),"},{"DocComment":"     "The origin is: Point { x: 0, y: 0 }","},{"DocComment":" );"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Manually implementing:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::fmt;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct Point {"},{"DocComment":"     x: i32,"},{"DocComment":"     y: i32,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl fmt::Debug for Point {"},{"DocComment":"     fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {"},{"DocComment":"         f.debug_struct("Point")"},{"DocComment":"          .field("x", &self.x)"},{"DocComment":"          .field("y", &self.y)"},{"DocComment":"          .finish()"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let origin = Point { x: 0, y: 0 };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!("},{"DocComment":"     format!("The origin is: {origin:?}"),"},{"DocComment":"     "The origin is: Point { x: 0, y: 0 }","},{"DocComment":" );"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are a number of helper methods on the [`Formatter`] struct to help you with manual"},{"DocComment":" implementations, such as [`debug_struct`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`debug_struct`]: Formatter::debug_struct"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Types that do not wish to use the standard suite of debug representations"},{"DocComment":" provided by the `Formatter` trait (`debug_struct`, `debug_tuple`,"},{"DocComment":" `debug_list`, `debug_set`, `debug_map`) can do something totally custom by"},{"DocComment":" manually writing an arbitrary representation to the `Formatter`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # use std::fmt;"},{"DocComment":" # struct Point {"},{"DocComment":" #     x: i32,"},{"DocComment":" #     y: i32,"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" #"},{"DocComment":" impl fmt::Debug for Point {"},{"DocComment":"     fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {"},{"DocComment":"         write!(f, "Point [{} {}]", self.x, self.y)"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `Debug` implementations using either `derive` or the debug builder API"},{"DocComment":" on [`Formatter`] support pretty-printing using the alternate flag: `{:#?}`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Pretty-printing with `#?`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" #[derive(Debug)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Point {"},{"DocComment":"     x: i32,"},{"DocComment":"     y: i32,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let origin = Point { x: 0, y: 0 };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let expected = "The origin is: Point {"},{"DocComment":"     x: 0,"},{"DocComment":"     y: 0,"},{"DocComment":" }";"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(format!("The origin is: {origin:#?}"), expected);"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"Debug"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Self"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"implied_clauses":[],"consts":[],"types":[],"methods":[{"params":{"regions":[{"index":0,"name":null,"mutability":"Unknown"},{"index":1,"name":null,"mutability":"Unknown"},{"index":2,"name":null,"mutability":"Unknown"}],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"skip_binder":{"name":"fmt","item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["fmt",0]},{"Ident":["Debug",0]},{"Ident":["fmt",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":22,"beg":{"line":1084,"col":4},"end":{"line":1084,"col":51}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":"Formats the value using the given formatter.

# Errors

This function should return [`Err`] if, and only if, the provided [`Formatter`] returns [`Err`].
String formatting is considered an infallible operation; this function only
returns a [`Result`] because writing to the underlying stream might fail and it must
provide a way to propagate the fact that an error has occurred back up the stack.
"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::fmt;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct Position {"},{"DocComment":"     longitude: f32,"},{"DocComment":"     latitude: f32,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl fmt::Debug for Position {"},{"DocComment":"     fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {"},{"DocComment":"         f.debug_tuple("")"},{"DocComment":"          .field(&self.longitude)"},{"DocComment":"          .field(&self.latitude)"},{"DocComment":"          .finish()"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let position = Position { longitude: 1.987, latitude: 2.983 };"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(format!("{position:?}"), "(1.987, 2.983)");"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(format!("{position:#?}"), "("},{"DocComment":"     1.987,"},{"DocComment":"     2.983,"},{"DocComment":" )");"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","is_variadic":false,"inputs":[{"Deduplicated":4660},{"HashConsedValue":[4676,{"Ref":[{"Var":{"Bound":[0,1]}},{"HashConsedValue":[4675,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":35},"generics":{"regions":[{"Var":{"Bound":[0,2]}}],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]},"Mut"]}]}],"output":{"HashConsedValue":[4678,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":3},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":549},{"HashConsedValue":[4677,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":36},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}},"default":null},"kind":{"TraitMethod":[12,0]}}],"vtable":{"id":{"Adt":34},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}},{"def_id":13,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["cmp",0]},{"Ident":["Eq",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":17,"beg":{"line":338,"col":0},"end":{"line":338,"col":58}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Trait for comparisons corresponding to [equivalence relations]("},{"DocComment":" https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equivalence_relation)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The primary difference to [`PartialEq`] is the additional requirement for reflexivity. A type"},{"DocComment":" that implements [`PartialEq`] guarantees that for all `a`, `b` and `c`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" - symmetric: `a == b` implies `b == a`"},{"DocComment":" - transitive: `a == b` and `b == c` implies `a == c`"},{"DocComment":" - consistent: `a != b` if and only if `!(a == b)`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `Eq`, which builds on top of [`PartialEq`] also implies:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" - reflexive: `a == a`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This property cannot be checked by the compiler, and therefore `Eq` is a trait without methods."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Violating this property is a logic error. The behavior resulting from a logic error is not"},{"DocComment":" specified, but users of the trait must ensure that such logic errors do *not* result in"},{"DocComment":" undefined behavior. This means that `unsafe` code **must not** rely on the correctness of these"},{"DocComment":" methods."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Floating point types such as [`f32`] and [`f64`] implement only [`PartialEq`] but *not* `Eq`"},{"DocComment":" because `NaN` != `NaN`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Derivable"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This trait can be used with `#[derive]`. When `derive`d, because `Eq` has no extra methods, it"},{"DocComment":" is only informing the compiler that this is an equivalence relation rather than a partial"},{"DocComment":" equivalence relation. Note that the `derive` strategy requires all fields are `Eq`, which isn't"},{"DocComment":" always desired."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## How can I implement `Eq`?"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you cannot use the `derive` strategy, specify that your type implements `Eq`, which has no"},{"DocComment":" extra methods:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" enum BookFormat {"},{"DocComment":"     Paperback,"},{"DocComment":"     Hardback,"},{"DocComment":"     Ebook,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct Book {"},{"DocComment":"     isbn: i32,"},{"DocComment":"     format: BookFormat,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl PartialEq for Book {"},{"DocComment":"     fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {"},{"DocComment":"         self.isbn == other.isbn"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl Eq for Book {}"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"Eq"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Self"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"implied_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":17,"beg":{"line":338,"col":20},"end":{"line":338,"col":43}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnTrait","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":10,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":4630},{"Deduplicated":4630}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"consts":[],"types":[],"methods":[null,null],"vtable":null},{"def_id":14,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["hash",0]},{"Ident":["Hash",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":23,"beg":{"line":185,"col":0},"end":{"line":185,"col":36}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A hashable type."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Types implementing `Hash` are able to be [`hash`]ed with an instance of"},{"DocComment":" [`Hasher`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Implementing `Hash`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" You can derive `Hash` with `#[derive(Hash)]` if all fields implement `Hash`."},{"DocComment":" The resulting hash will be the combination of the values from calling"},{"DocComment":" [`hash`] on each field."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" #[derive(Hash)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Rustacean {"},{"DocComment":"     name: String,"},{"DocComment":"     country: String,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you need more control over how a value is hashed, you can of course"},{"DocComment":" implement the `Hash` trait yourself:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher};"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct Person {"},{"DocComment":"     id: u32,"},{"DocComment":"     name: String,"},{"DocComment":"     phone: u64,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl Hash for Person {"},{"DocComment":"     fn hash<H: Hasher>(&self, state: &mut H) {"},{"DocComment":"         self.id.hash(state);"},{"DocComment":"         self.phone.hash(state);"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## `Hash` and `Eq`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When implementing both `Hash` and [`Eq`], it is important that the following"},{"DocComment":" property holds:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```text"},{"DocComment":" k1 == k2 -> hash(k1) == hash(k2)"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In other words, if two keys are equal, their hashes must also be equal."},{"DocComment":" [`HashMap`] and [`HashSet`] both rely on this behavior."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Thankfully, you won't need to worry about upholding this property when"},{"DocComment":" deriving both [`Eq`] and `Hash` with `#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Violating this property is a logic error. The behavior resulting from a logic error is not"},{"DocComment":" specified, but users of the trait must ensure that such logic errors do *not* result in"},{"DocComment":" undefined behavior. This means that `unsafe` code **must not** rely on the correctness of these"},{"DocComment":" methods."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Prefix collisions"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Implementations of `hash` should ensure that the data they"},{"DocComment":" pass to the `Hasher` are prefix-free. That is,"},{"DocComment":" values which are not equal should cause two different sequences of values to be written,"},{"DocComment":" and neither of the two sequences should be a prefix of the other."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For example, the standard implementation of [`Hash` for `&str`][impl] passes an extra"},{"DocComment":" `0xFF` byte to the `Hasher` so that the values `("ab", "c")` and `("a","},{"DocComment":" "bc")` hash differently."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Portability"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Due to differences in endianness and type sizes, data fed by `Hash` to a `Hasher`"},{"DocComment":" should not be considered portable across platforms. Additionally the data passed by most"},{"DocComment":" standard library types should not be considered stable between compiler versions."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This means tests shouldn't probe hard-coded hash values or data fed to a `Hasher` and"},{"DocComment":" instead should check consistency with `Eq`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Serialization formats intended to be portable between platforms or compiler versions should"},{"DocComment":" either avoid encoding hashes or only rely on `Hash` and `Hasher` implementations that"},{"DocComment":" provide additional guarantees."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`HashMap`]: ../../std/collections/struct.HashMap.html"},{"DocComment":" [`HashSet`]: ../../std/collections/struct.HashSet.html"},{"DocComment":" [`hash`]: Hash::hash"},{"DocComment":" [impl]: ../../std/primitive.str.html#impl-Hash-for-str"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"Hash"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Self"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"implied_clauses":[],"consts":[],"types":[],"methods":[{"params":{"regions":[{"index":0,"name":null,"mutability":"Unknown"},{"index":1,"name":null,"mutability":"Unknown"}],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"H"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":23,"beg":{"line":198,"col":15},"end":{"line":198,"col":21}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnFn","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":18,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":417}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"skip_binder":{"name":"hash","item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["hash",0]},{"Ident":["Hash",0]},{"Ident":["hash",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":23,"beg":{"line":198,"col":4},"end":{"line":198,"col":45}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Feeds this value into the given [`Hasher`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::hash::{DefaultHasher, Hash, Hasher};"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut hasher = DefaultHasher::new();"},{"DocComment":" 7920.hash(&mut hasher);"},{"DocComment":" println!("Hash is {:x}!", hasher.finish());"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","is_variadic":false,"inputs":[{"Deduplicated":4660},{"HashConsedValue":[4679,{"Ref":[{"Var":{"Bound":[0,1]}},{"Deduplicated":495},"Mut"]}]}],"output":{"Deduplicated":549}},"default":null},"kind":{"TraitMethod":[14,0]}},null],"vtable":null},{"def_id":15,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["Send",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":11,"beg":{"line":92,"col":0},"end":{"line":92,"col":26}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Types that can be transferred across thread boundaries."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This trait is automatically implemented when the compiler determines it's"},{"DocComment":" appropriate."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" An example of a non-`Send` type is the reference-counting pointer"},{"DocComment":" [`rc::Rc`][`Rc`]. If two threads attempt to clone [`Rc`]s that point to the same"},{"DocComment":" reference-counted value, they might try to update the reference count at the"},{"DocComment":" same time, which is [undefined behavior][ub] because [`Rc`] doesn't use atomic"},{"DocComment":" operations. Its cousin [`sync::Arc`][arc] does use atomic operations (incurring"},{"DocComment":" some overhead) and thus is `Send`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" See [the Nomicon](../../nomicon/send-and-sync.html) and the [`Sync`] trait for more details."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`Rc`]: ../../std/rc/struct.Rc.html"},{"DocComment":" [arc]: ../../std/sync/struct.Arc.html"},{"DocComment":" [ub]: ../../reference/behavior-considered-undefined.html"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"Send"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Self"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"implied_clauses":[],"consts":[],"types":[],"methods":[],"vtable":{"id":{"Adt":37},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}},{"def_id":16,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["Sync",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":11,"beg":{"line":657,"col":0},"end":{"line":657,"col":26}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Types for which it is safe to share references between threads."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This trait is automatically implemented when the compiler determines"},{"DocComment":" it's appropriate."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The precise definition is: a type `T` is [`Sync`] if and only if `&T` is"},{"DocComment":" [`Send`]. In other words, if there is no possibility of"},{"DocComment":" [undefined behavior][ub] (including data races) when passing"},{"DocComment":" `&T` references between threads."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" As one would expect, primitive types like [`u8`] and [`f64`]"},{"DocComment":" are all [`Sync`], and so are simple aggregate types containing them,"},{"DocComment":" like tuples, structs and enums. More examples of basic [`Sync`]"},{"DocComment":" types include "immutable" types like `&T`, and those with simple"},{"DocComment":" inherited mutability, such as [`Box<T>`][box], [`Vec<T>`][vec] and"},{"DocComment":" most other collection types. (Generic parameters need to be [`Sync`]"},{"DocComment":" for their container to be [`Sync`].)"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" A somewhat surprising consequence of the definition is that `&mut T`"},{"DocComment":" is `Sync` (if `T` is `Sync`) even though it seems like that might"},{"DocComment":" provide unsynchronized mutation. The trick is that a mutable"},{"DocComment":" reference behind a shared reference (that is, `& &mut T`)"},{"DocComment":" becomes read-only, as if it were a `& &T`. Hence there is no risk"},{"DocComment":" of a data race."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" A shorter overview of how [`Sync`] and [`Send`] relate to referencing:"},{"DocComment":" * `&T` is [`Send`] if and only if `T` is [`Sync`]"},{"DocComment":" * `&mut T` is [`Send`] if and only if `T` is [`Send`]"},{"DocComment":" * `&T` and `&mut T` are [`Sync`] if and only if `T` is [`Sync`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Types that are not `Sync` are those that have "interior"},{"DocComment":" mutability" in a non-thread-safe form, such as [`Cell`][cell]"},{"DocComment":" and [`RefCell`][refcell]. These types allow for mutation of"},{"DocComment":" their contents even through an immutable, shared reference. For"},{"DocComment":" example the `set` method on [`Cell<T>`][cell] takes `&self`, so it requires"},{"DocComment":" only a shared reference [`&Cell<T>`][cell]. The method performs no"},{"DocComment":" synchronization, thus [`Cell`][cell] cannot be `Sync`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Another example of a non-`Sync` type is the reference-counting"},{"DocComment":" pointer [`Rc`][rc]. Given any reference [`&Rc<T>`][rc], you can clone"},{"DocComment":" a new [`Rc<T>`][rc], modifying the reference counts in a non-atomic way."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For cases when one does need thread-safe interior mutability,"},{"DocComment":" Rust provides [atomic data types], as well as explicit locking via"},{"DocComment":" [`sync::Mutex`][mutex] and [`sync::RwLock`][rwlock]. These types"},{"DocComment":" ensure that any mutation cannot cause data races, hence the types"},{"DocComment":" are `Sync`. Likewise, [`sync::Arc`][arc] provides a thread-safe"},{"DocComment":" analogue of [`Rc`][rc]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Any types with interior mutability must also use the"},{"DocComment":" [`cell::UnsafeCell`][unsafecell] wrapper around the value(s) which"},{"DocComment":" can be mutated through a shared reference. Failing to doing this is"},{"DocComment":" [undefined behavior][ub]. For example, [`transmute`][transmute]-ing"},{"DocComment":" from `&T` to `&mut T` is invalid."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" See [the Nomicon][nomicon-send-and-sync] for more details about `Sync`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [box]: ../../std/boxed/struct.Box.html"},{"DocComment":" [vec]: ../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html"},{"DocComment":" [cell]: crate::cell::Cell"},{"DocComment":" [refcell]: crate::cell::RefCell"},{"DocComment":" [rc]: ../../std/rc/struct.Rc.html"},{"DocComment":" [arc]: ../../std/sync/struct.Arc.html"},{"DocComment":" [atomic data types]: crate::sync::atomic"},{"DocComment":" [mutex]: ../../std/sync/struct.Mutex.html"},{"DocComment":" [rwlock]: ../../std/sync/struct.RwLock.html"},{"DocComment":" [unsafecell]: crate::cell::UnsafeCell"},{"DocComment":" [ub]: ../../reference/behavior-considered-undefined.html"},{"DocComment":" [transmute]: crate::mem::transmute"},{"DocComment":" [nomicon-send-and-sync]: ../../nomicon/send-and-sync.html"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"sync"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Self"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"implied_clauses":[],"consts":[],"types":[],"methods":[],"vtable":{"id":{"Adt":38},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}},{"def_id":17,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["Unpin",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":11,"beg":{"line":1014,"col":0},"end":{"line":1014,"col":20}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Types that do not require any pinning guarantees."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For information on what "pinning" is, see the [`pin` module] documentation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Implementing the `Unpin` trait for `T` expresses the fact that `T` is pinning-agnostic:"},{"DocComment":" it shall not expose nor rely on any pinning guarantees. This, in turn, means that a"},{"DocComment":" `Pin`-wrapped pointer to such a type can feature a *fully unrestricted* API."},{"DocComment":" In other words, if `T: Unpin`, a value of type `T` will *not* be bound by the invariants"},{"DocComment":" which pinning otherwise offers, even when "pinned" by a [`Pin<Ptr>`] pointing at it."},{"DocComment":" When a value of type `T` is pointed at by a [`Pin<Ptr>`], [`Pin`] will not restrict access"},{"DocComment":" to the pointee value like it normally would, thus allowing the user to do anything that they"},{"DocComment":" normally could with a non-[`Pin`]-wrapped `Ptr` to that value."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The idea of this trait is to alleviate the reduced ergonomics of APIs that require the use"},{"DocComment":" of [`Pin`] for soundness for some types, but which also want to be used by other types that"},{"DocComment":" don't care about pinning. The prime example of such an API is [`Future::poll`]. There are many"},{"DocComment":" [`Future`] types that don't care about pinning. These futures can implement `Unpin` and"},{"DocComment":" therefore get around the pinning related restrictions in the API, while still allowing the"},{"DocComment":" subset of [`Future`]s which *do* require pinning to be implemented soundly."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For more discussion on the consequences of [`Unpin`] within the wider scope of the pinning"},{"DocComment":" system, see the [section about `Unpin`] in the [`pin` module]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `Unpin` has no consequence at all for non-pinned data. In particular, [`mem::replace`] happily"},{"DocComment":" moves `!Unpin` data, which would be immovable when pinned ([`mem::replace`] works for any"},{"DocComment":" `&mut T`, not just when `T: Unpin`)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" *However*, you cannot use [`mem::replace`] on `!Unpin` data which is *pinned* by being wrapped"},{"DocComment":" inside a [`Pin<Ptr>`] pointing at it. This is because you cannot (safely) use a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] to get a `&mut T` to its pointee value, which you would need to call"},{"DocComment":" [`mem::replace`], and *that* is what makes this system work."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" So this, for example, can only be done on types implementing `Unpin`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(unused_must_use)]"},{"DocComment":"